On Tue, 25 Jun 2002, Brad Chapman wrote:

>      I have just started using SELinux on my 2.4.18 system. I found an enormous
> number of avc: denied messages in my dmesg and attempted to remove them. So far
> I have tweaked the devfs_contexts and the policy.conf files (using
> scripts/newrules.pl for policy.conf) since most of the errors were related to
> device files and system call commands. I have also installed the SELinux devfsd
> extension and added the config entries to my devfsd.conf, to no apparent avail.
>
>      What is the fastest way to eliminate most (if not preferably all) of these
> messages and put me closer to activating enforcing mode?

The Configuring the SELinux Policy report discusses how to customize your
policy, although this is certainly not a fast process.  The report is
included in the distribution and is also available from the NSA SELinux
web site.

It sounds like you are using Russell Coker's Debian selinux package rather
than the upstream distribution.  Is that correct?  Russell's package
includes a more complete example policy configuration.

You should be cautious about simply adding rules generated by newrules.pl.
It can be a helpful tool if used properly, but you should not blindly add
the rules it generates to your policy configuration.  In many cases, you
will want to define new domains and/or types rather than granting
permissions to the existing ones in order to preserve your security goals.

The SELinux devfsd support has known problems with preserving security
contexts on devfs entries.  Fully supporting the SELinux devfsd will
require modifications to the kernel devfs code.  I'd suggest using
devfs_contexts instead.

